            "<strong>Fibroids</strong> (leiomyomas) are benign smooth-muscle tumours of the uterus, oestrogen- and progesterone-dependent, found in up to 70 to 80 per cent of women of African descent by the age of 50 — earlier, larger, more numerous and more symptomatic than in other populations — and they are the leading indication for hysterectomy and myomectomy in Ghana's hospitals. They are classified by position: <strong>submucosal</strong> (bulging into the cavity; the smallest cause the heaviest bleeding and the most infertility and miscarriage), <strong>intramural</strong> (within the wall; the commonest), <strong>subserosal</strong> (projecting outwards, sometimes on a stalk, pressing on bladder and bowel), and cervical or broad-ligament. Most are asymptomatic and found on examination or scan; the symptomatic woman has <strong>heavy, prolonged periods</strong> with clots and the iron-deficiency anaemia of lesson 2.1, <strong>pressure symptoms</strong> — urinary frequency, retention, constipation, a feeling of fullness, a visibly enlarged abdomen she may have assumed was pregnancy or weight — dysmenorrhoea, dyspareunia, backache, and <strong>subfertility or recurrent miscarriage</strong> when the cavity is distorted. Acute pain comes from <strong>torsion</strong> of a pedunculated fibroid or from <strong>red degeneration</strong>, the haemorrhagic infarction of a rapidly growing fibroid typically in the second trimester of pregnancy — severe localised pain, low-grade fever, vomiting and a tender fibroid, managed conservatively with rest, analgesia and fluids, not surgery. In pregnancy fibroids also increase miscarriage, malpresentation, placental abruption, preterm labour, obstructed labour (a cervical or lower-segment fibroid), caesarean section and postpartum haemorrhage. Examination finds a firm, irregular, non-tender, mobile mass continuous with the uterus, sized in weeks of pregnancy; <strong>ultrasound</strong> confirms number, size and position (and the ovaries, which must be seen separately, since a solid adnexal mass is a different problem), a full blood count measures the anaemia, and hysteroscopy or saline sonography maps submucosal fibroids before surgery. Sarcomatous change is very rare (well under 1 in 500) but rapid growth after the menopause, or in any woman, is investigated.",
            "Management depends on symptoms, size, fertility wishes and age, and <strong>asymptomatic fibroids need no treatment</strong> — only explanation and review, since they shrink after the menopause. Symptomatic bleeding without cavity distortion is managed as in lesson 2.1: tranexamic acid, NSAIDs, the levonorgestrel system (if the cavity allows), combined or progestogen-only hormones, and iron. <strong>GnRH analogues</strong> (goserelin, leuprorelin) for three to six months shrink fibroids by up to half and stop bleeding, allowing the haemoglobin to recover and the operation to be smaller — but they cause menopausal symptoms and bone loss, the fibroids regrow when stopped, and they are expensive; ulipristal acetate and the newer oral GnRH antagonists have limited availability. <strong>Myomectomy</strong> — removal of the fibroids with preservation of the uterus, open (abdominal), laparoscopic or hysteroscopic for submucosal ones — is the operation for the woman who wants children, with the counselling that blood loss can be heavy (blood is crossmatched and some units use a tourniquet, vasopressin or tranexamic acid), that adhesions may form, that fibroids recur in a quarter to half within five years, that she should wait six to twelve months before conceiving, and that a caesarean section may be advised if the cavity was entered. <strong>Hysterectomy</strong> is definitive for the woman whose family is complete, and the woman who chooses it must hear clearly that the ovaries are normally conserved, so there is no menopause, that her sexual function is not destroyed, and that she will not ‘become a man’ — beliefs that cause women to accept years of anaemia instead. <strong>Uterine artery embolisation</strong>, performed by interventional radiologists at a few centres, shrinks fibroids by blocking their blood supply and suits the woman who wants to avoid surgery and has completed her family. The nurse's role runs through all of it: correcting the anaemia before the operation (iron, sometimes transfusion), the pre- and post-operative care of lesson 7.1, and the frank conversation that lets a woman choose from accurate information rather than from the fears of the market.",
