            "An <strong>adnexal mass</strong> is a swelling of the ovary, tube or adjacent structures, and the first questions are whether it is physiological, whether it is causing an emergency and whether it might be malignant. <strong>Functional cysts</strong> — follicular (a follicle that did not rupture) and corpus luteum cysts — are the commonest in women of reproductive age, are usually under 5 cm, simple, thin-walled and unilocular on scan, cause no symptoms or mild pain, and resolve within two or three cycles; they are rescanned at six to twelve weeks, not operated on, and not treated with the combined pill, which does not hasten resolution. <strong>Pathological benign cysts</strong> include the <strong>dermoid cyst</strong> (mature cystic teratoma — the commonest ovarian tumour in young women, containing hair, teeth, sebum and bone, bilateral in a tenth, and the one most prone to torsion), the <strong>endometrioma</strong> (a ‘chocolate cyst’ of old blood in endometriosis, with dysmenorrhoea, dyspareunia and infertility), <strong>serous and mucinous cystadenomas</strong> (which may grow very large; the mucinous cyst filling the abdomen of a thin woman is a classic Ghanaian presentation), fibromas and theca-lutein cysts in molar pregnancy and ovulation induction. In <strong>polycystic ovaries</strong> the many small follicles are a sign, not cysts needing removal. The assessment is history (pain, its onset and character, bloating, early satiety, weight loss, urinary frequency, menstrual changes, family history of breast or ovarian cancer), examination (a mobile mass separate from the uterus, ascites, fixed irregular masses and nodules in the pouch of Douglas suggesting malignancy), a <strong>pregnancy test</strong>, <strong>ultrasound</strong>, and <strong>CA-125</strong> in the post-menopausal woman or where the scan is suspicious — remembering that CA-125 rises in fibroids, endometriosis, infection and pregnancy, so it is not a screening test in young women. The <strong>Risk of Malignancy Index</strong> combines the ultrasound score (multilocular, solid areas, bilateral, ascites, metastases), menopausal status and CA-125, and a score above 200 to 250 sends the woman to a gynaecological oncologist; in a woman under 40 with a complex mass, alpha-fetoprotein, hCG and lactate dehydrogenase screen for germ cell tumours (lesson 6.2). Simple cysts under 5 cm are observed; persistent, enlarging, symptomatic or complex cysts are removed by laparoscopic or open <strong>cystectomy</strong> (preserving the ovary in the young woman) or oophorectomy, with the specimen sent whole for histology.",
