            "<strong>Pelvic organ prolapse</strong> is the descent of the pelvic organs through the vagina when the pelvic floor muscles and the fascial supports are stretched or torn — by vaginal births, especially prolonged labour, large babies and instrumental deliveries; by chronic straining from constipation, chronic cough and heavy carrying (the woman who has carried water, firewood and farm produce on her head for forty years); by obesity; and by the loss of oestrogen after the menopause, which is when most women present. The types are a <strong>cystocele</strong> (bladder through the anterior wall), <strong>urethrocele</strong>, <strong>rectocele</strong> (rectum through the posterior wall), <strong>enterocele</strong> (small bowel through the posterior fornix), <strong>uterine prolapse</strong> (first degree within the vagina, second degree to the introitus, third degree beyond it, and <strong>procidentia</strong> when the whole uterus lies outside the body), and <strong>vault prolapse</strong> after hysterectomy; the POP-Q system stages descent from 0 to 4 against the hymen. The woman complains of ‘something coming down’, a dragging ache or heaviness worse at the end of the day and relieved by lying down, a lump she can feel or see, difficulty emptying the bladder or bowel (she may need to push the bulge back to pass urine or stool), stress incontinence or recurrent urinary infection, discomfort with intercourse, and in procidentia a dry, ulcerated, bleeding, infected cervix and sometimes obstructed ureters. Examination is in the Sims position with a Sims speculum, asking her to cough and strain, and also standing if the prolapse is not reproduced; urinalysis and a post-void residual check the bladder, and renal function is tested in procidentia.",
