            "<strong>Urinary incontinence</strong> — any involuntary leakage of urine — affects a quarter to a third of adult women, most of whom never mention it. <strong>Stress incontinence</strong> is leakage with coughing, sneezing, laughing, lifting or running, from a weak pelvic floor and urethral sphincter after childbirth and the menopause; <strong>urge incontinence</strong> (overactive bladder) is leakage preceded by a sudden compelling urge, with frequency and nocturia, from an unstable detrusor muscle, worsened by caffeine, infection, atrophy and neurological disease; most women have a <strong>mixed</strong> picture. <strong>Overflow incontinence</strong> is the dribbling of a chronically full bladder that cannot empty — after obstructed labour, with a large prolapse or fibroid, in diabetic neuropathy, or after surgery — and is diagnosed by a large post-void residual; <strong>continuous incontinence</strong>, day and night, lying and standing, is a fistula until proved otherwise. The assessment is the history, a <strong>three-day bladder diary</strong> (times, volumes, fluid intake, leaks and their triggers), urinalysis and culture, a cough test with a full bladder, examination for prolapse and atrophy, a post-void residual by catheter or scan, and urodynamic studies before surgery where available. Treatment begins conservatively for every type: treat infection, atrophy and constipation; reduce caffeine, carbonated drinks and evening fluids without restricting total intake; weight loss; <strong>pelvic floor training</strong> for three months as the first-line treatment of stress incontinence; <strong>bladder training</strong> for urge incontinence — voiding by the clock and lengthening the interval by 15 minutes a week, with distraction and pelvic floor contraction to suppress the urge, until she holds for three to four hours; and then antimuscarinic drugs (oxybutynin, solifenacin — dry mouth, constipation and confusion in the elderly) or mirabegron for the overactive bladder, duloxetine occasionally for stress incontinence, and surgery (mid-urethral slings, colposuspension, bulking agents) for stress incontinence that fails conservative treatment. Containment — pads, absorbent underwear, a well-managed indwelling or intermittent catheter when nothing else is possible — is care, not failure. The woman who smells of urine withdraws from church, market and marriage; the nurse who asks the question she will not volunteer gives her back those three things.",
